CloudBurrow patch 13 to github.com/goccy/bigquery-emulator v0.8.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1310).

The bug: a table's creationTime and lastModifiedTime, and a job's
statistics.creationTime, startTime and endTime, were seconds since the
epoch (time.Now().Unix(), startTime.Unix()), where BigQuery gives them
"in milliseconds since the epoch"
(https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/rest/v2/tables#Table,
https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/rest/v2/Job#JobStatistics).
Measured: tables.get answered "creationTime":"1792085781" for a table just
made, which the Go client's TableMetadata.CreationTime read as January
1970. A dataset was given no creationTime or lastModifiedTime at all.

The patch gives them in milliseconds (UnixMilli): tables.insert's and the
tables a server loads from a source (types.Table.ToBigqueryV2), every job
the emulator runs (a load from Cloud Storage, an extract, a query job and
jobs.query), and datasets.insert, which now sets both of a dataset's
times, as they are output only. A table or job written by an engine
without this patch keeps its seconds.

